1
00:00:04,500 --> 00:00:07,854
A colleague's laptop is handed
to you with that sentence.

2
00:00:07,894 --> 00:00:12,498
Nothing is broken. An earlier git pull,
which fetches from the server and then

3
00:00:12,538 --> 00:00:16,011
merges, stopped at a conflict
three weeks ago and was left.

4
00:00:16,051 --> 00:00:19,881
A conflict is a place where Git
can't decide between two changes.

5
00:00:20,221 --> 00:00:24,948
Since then the repository has been in the
middle of a merge, through reboots and a

6
00:00:24,988 --> 00:00:29,599
holiday, and every refusal Git printed
was a correct statement about that state.

7
00:00:29,639 --> 00:00:34,424
Keep the laptop's sentence in mind. Later
you'll watch Git print the refusal behind

8
00:00:34,464 --> 00:00:34,599
it.

9
00:00:34,939 --> 00:00:39,470
The diagnosis is one git status, or one
listing of the .git directory, where Git

10
00:00:39,510 --> 00:00:44,156
keeps the repository. The decision that
follows isn't automatic, and that is where

11
00:00:44,196 --> 00:00:48,670
people do damage: continue, abort, or
anchor and then abort are three different

12
00:00:48,710 --> 00:00:51,928
outcomes for the work that was
done inside the operation.

13
00:00:53,768 --> 00:00:57,759
Welcome back to Git and GitHub
Deep Mastery. Pull up a chair.

14
00:00:57,799 --> 00:01:02,782
In video 180 the root cause was an
unfinished rebase, and you read it from a

15
00:01:02,822 --> 00:01:08,202
directory. Today you generalize: five
operations can stop half-way, merge, rebase,

16
00:01:08,242 --> 00:01:12,167
cherry-pick, revert and bisect,
and each leaves a signature.

17
00:01:12,507 --> 00:01:17,045
A merge joins two lines of history. A
rebase copies commits onto a new base.

18
00:01:17,085 --> 00:01:20,961
A cherry-pick copies one commit's
change onto the current branch.

19
00:01:21,001 --> 00:01:26,021
A revert adds a commit that undoes an earlier
one. And a bisect searches the history

20
00:01:26,061 --> 00:01:28,310
for the commit that changed something.

21
00:01:28,650 --> 00:01:33,680
You've seen each of them individually: the
sequencer in video 62, bisect in video

22
00:01:33,720 --> 00:01:38,750
71. This video puts them side by side so
that you can take over a repository that

23
00:01:38,790 --> 00:01:44,007
somebody else left in the middle of something
and say within seconds which operation

24
00:01:44,047 --> 00:01:46,636
it is, where it started, and what remains.

25
00:01:46,976 --> 00:01:51,828
The format is a quiz. For each operation
I show you the listing of .git first.

26
00:01:51,868 --> 00:01:56,218
You name the operation. Then we look
at what git status says about it.

27
00:01:58,058 --> 00:01:59,637
Here's what you'll walk away with.

28
00:01:59,977 --> 00:02:01,389
After this video you can:

29
00:02:01,729 --> 00:02:06,927
Tell from .git alone whether a merge, a
rebase, a cherry-pick, a revert or a bisect

30
00:02:06,967 --> 00:02:07,874
is in progress.

31
00:02:08,214 --> 00:02:11,985
Say for each which refs and
files exist and where HEAD is.

32
00:02:12,325 --> 00:02:15,872
Name the ways out of each operation
and what each leaves behind.

33
00:02:16,212 --> 00:02:19,673
Explain what commands refuse
to run in each state and why.

34
00:02:20,013 --> 00:02:23,898
Take over a repository that somebody
else left in the middle of something.

35
00:02:25,738 --> 00:02:30,549
In one sentence. Merge, rebase, cherry-pick,
revert and bisect can stop half-way,

36
00:02:30,589 --> 00:02:35,101
and each leaves named files in .git that
say which operation it is, where it

37
00:02:35,141 --> 00:02:36,658
started, and what remains.

38
00:02:36,998 --> 00:02:41,659
Precisely. Git has no process that "is"
a rebase. An operation in progress is

39
00:02:41,699 --> 00:02:46,604
nothing but files: a pseudoref such as
MERGE_HEAD that holds a commit ID, and for

40
00:02:46,644 --> 00:02:49,718
multi-step operations a
directory with a todo list.

41
00:02:49,758 --> 00:02:53,747
git status reads those files and
prints its first lines from them.

42
00:02:54,087 --> 00:02:57,463
Three consequences follow, and
each one matters in an incident.

43
00:02:57,803 --> 00:03:01,045
A stopped operation survives a
reboot and can be weeks old.

44
00:03:01,385 --> 00:03:04,915
Deleting the files by hand ends
the operation without undoing it.

45
00:03:05,255 --> 00:03:08,982
And you can diagnose a repository
you were handed by listing .git.

46
00:03:09,322 --> 00:03:10,169
The signatures.

47
00:03:10,509 --> 00:03:15,284
Merge: MERGE_HEAD is the commit being
merged in. It becomes the second parent.

48
00:03:15,324 --> 00:03:19,791
MERGE_MSG is the prepared message.
ORIG_HEAD is the tip before the merge.

49
00:03:19,831 --> 00:03:23,803
AUTO_MERGE is a tree with the
conflict markers as Git wrote them.

50
00:03:23,843 --> 00:03:27,939
HEAD, Git's note of where you are,
is still attached to the branch.

51
00:03:28,279 --> 00:03:33,140
Rebase: a directory, rebase-merge, or
rebase-apply for the older apply backend, with

52
00:03:33,180 --> 00:03:37,691
the same role. HEAD is a raw commit ID.
REBASE_HEAD is the commit whose replay

53
00:03:37,731 --> 00:03:42,359
stopped. Inside the directory, head-name
is the branch that will be moved at the

54
00:03:42,399 --> 00:03:47,260
end, orig-head its tip at the start, onto
the new base, msgnum and end the position,

55
00:03:47,300 --> 00:03:49,594
and git-rebase-todo the remaining picks.

56
00:03:49,934 --> 00:03:53,275
Cherry-pick: CHERRY_PICK_HEAD
is the commit being applied.

57
00:03:53,315 --> 00:03:57,939
HEAD stays on the branch. A sequencer
directory exists when several commits were

58
00:03:57,979 --> 00:04:02,662
requested: head is where the branch was
before the first pick, and todo lists the

59
00:04:02,702 --> 00:04:07,501
current pick first and then those not yet
applied. A cherry-pick of a single commit

60
00:04:07,541 --> 00:04:09,075
has no sequencer directory.

61
00:04:09,415 --> 00:04:13,811
Revert: REVERT_HEAD is the commit being
undone. The rest is the same machinery as

62
00:04:13,851 --> 00:04:16,988
cherry-pick with the roles of
base and "theirs" exchanged.

63
00:04:17,328 --> 00:04:22,549
Bisect: BISECT_START is the branch to return
to, BISECT_LOG the replayable record,

64
00:04:22,589 --> 00:04:27,490
BISECT_TERMS the two words in use, and
the marks are refs under refs/bisect/.

65
00:04:27,530 --> 00:04:32,558
HEAD is detached on the commit under test.
A bisect has no conflict and a clean

66
00:04:32,598 --> 00:04:36,857
working tree, which is why it's the
operation most often forgotten.

67
00:04:37,197 --> 00:04:42,051
Two files aren't signatures. ORIG_HEAD and
FETCH_HEAD are records of past commands,

68
00:04:42,091 --> 00:04:43,938
not of an operation in progress.

69
00:04:44,278 --> 00:04:47,902
The exits. Each operation has
up to three kinds of exit.

70
00:04:47,942 --> 00:04:53,399
Continue. Leave and restore the starting
point. Or leave and keep the current state.

71
00:04:53,739 --> 00:04:58,773
Every "leave and restore" command resets
the index, which is the proposed next

72
00:04:58,813 --> 00:05:03,197
commit, and the working tree, the
files you edit, for tracked files.

73
00:05:03,237 --> 00:05:06,840
So uncommitted edits made
during the operation are lost.

74
00:05:06,880 --> 00:05:12,304
Section 29.6 marks them all 🟡. The chapter's
command safety table, section 29.14, is

75
00:05:12,344 --> 00:05:17,768
stricter about one of them, and this video
follows the table: git merge --abort is 🔴

76
00:05:17,808 --> 00:05:23,167
DANGEROUS, because it destroys every
resolution made so far and edits staged during

77
00:05:23,207 --> 00:05:28,306
the merge, and the manual warns that
uncommitted changes present when the merge

78
00:05:28,346 --> 00:05:30,778
started can't always be reconstructed.

79
00:05:31,118 --> 00:05:37,615
git rebase --abort, git cherry-pick --abort,
git revert --abort and git bisect reset

80
00:05:37,655 --> 00:05:38,704
are 🟡 CAUTION.

81
00:05:39,044 --> 00:05:42,716
When not to reach for an exit.
The decision isn't automatic.

82
00:05:42,756 --> 00:05:46,923
Continue, if the operation was
intended and the resolution is known.

83
00:05:46,963 --> 00:05:52,120
Abort, if nothing done inside it matters.
Anchor and then abort, as in video 180, if

84
00:05:52,160 --> 00:05:56,946
commits were made inside it. To anchor
is to give those commits a branch name.

85
00:05:56,986 --> 00:05:59,297
Ask what was intended before choosing.

86
00:06:01,137 --> 00:06:05,961
A picture helps. The textbook's analogy is
a surgeon's instrument count: the list on

87
00:06:06,001 --> 00:06:10,710
the wall says what is still inside the
patient. You don't close until the count is

88
00:06:10,750 --> 00:06:14,185
right, and anyone who walks into
the room can read the list.

89
00:06:14,525 --> 00:06:18,288
The analogy breaks because Git's
list is also the undo record.

90
00:06:18,328 --> 00:06:23,195
The same files that describe the operation
are what --abort reads to restore the

91
00:06:23,235 --> 00:06:28,226
starting point. Delete the list by hand,
and you haven't finished the operation or

92
00:06:28,266 --> 00:06:32,274
undone it. You've made it impossible
to do either with Git's help.

93
00:06:32,614 --> 00:06:36,153
So hold two questions for any
repository you're handed.

94
00:06:36,193 --> 00:06:39,861
First: which state files exist?
That names the operation.

95
00:06:39,901 --> 00:06:45,197
Second: is HEAD a branch name or a raw
commit ID? That tells you where new commits

96
00:06:45,237 --> 00:06:45,782
would go.

97
00:06:47,622 --> 00:06:53,579
Quick quiz before the table. Two of the
five operations detach HEAD, so that a new

98
00:06:53,619 --> 00:06:57,821
commit lands on no branch.
Which two? A, merge and rebase.

99
00:06:57,861 --> 00:07:02,282
B, rebase and bisect. C, cherry-pick
and revert. Your answer?

100
00:07:19,122 --> 00:07:23,637
B. Point at the HEAD column when the table
is complete: two operations detach HEAD,

101
00:07:23,677 --> 00:07:28,247
rebase and bisect. Those are the two in
which a commit made "on the branch" isn't on

102
00:07:28,287 --> 00:07:28,850
the branch.

103
00:07:30,690 --> 00:07:32,769
The reference: a repository at rest.

104
00:07:36,109 --> 00:07:40,141
Memorize this listing. .git/HEAD
contains ref: refs/heads/main.

105
00:07:40,181 --> 00:07:45,053
Any file in capitals that isn't in this
listing was left by a command: by an

106
00:07:45,093 --> 00:07:48,738
operation still in progress,
or by one that has finished.

107
00:07:49,078 --> 00:07:54,714
Try it now. Thirty seconds. In the lab
shell, or in any repository you have, run ls

108
00:07:54,754 --> 00:07:59,911
.git, which only reads. Is there a name
in capitals that this listing lacks?

109
00:07:59,951 --> 00:08:01,005
Say it out loud.

110
00:08:05,845 --> 00:08:10,578
If you found ORIG_HEAD or FETCH_HEAD,
those two are records of past commands.

111
00:08:10,618 --> 00:08:15,660
Any other new name is worth holding on to,
because the next five listings tell you

112
00:08:15,700 --> 00:08:16,528
what it means.

113
00:08:16,868 --> 00:08:22,150
Repository two. Files first. Look at the
listing and name the operation before I

114
00:08:22,190 --> 00:08:23,215
show the status.

115
00:08:23,555 --> 00:08:25,943
Which operation is it? Say it out loud.

116
00:08:30,783 --> 00:08:36,213
MERGE_HEAD. A merge. The message file names
the branch being merged and lists the

117
00:08:36,253 --> 00:08:41,614
conflict. git ls-files -u shows three
stages for guard.yaml: base, ours, theirs.

118
00:08:41,654 --> 00:08:42,627
Now the status.

119
00:08:42,967 --> 00:08:47,953
git status doesn't print the word "merging".
It says "You have unmerged paths" and

120
00:08:47,993 --> 00:08:52,980
offers git merge --abort. After the last
conflict is staged, the text becomes "All

121
00:08:53,020 --> 00:08:57,944
conflicts fixed but you are still merging".
The first line says "On branch main":

122
00:08:57,984 --> 00:08:58,986
HEAD is attached.

123
00:08:59,326 --> 00:09:05,024
Repository three. Name it from the
files. Predict what .git/HEAD contains.

124
00:09:08,364 --> 00:09:13,658
The directory rebase-merge: a rebase. HEAD
is a raw commit ID, and it equals onto.

125
00:09:13,698 --> 00:09:16,976
Position 1 of 3. Two picks
remain in the todo file.

126
00:09:17,016 --> 00:09:20,878
And head-name tells you which
branch will move when it ends.

127
00:09:21,218 --> 00:09:25,879
The status prints all of that in words, with
the three exits: continue, skip, abort.

128
00:09:26,219 --> 00:09:27,321
Repository four.

129
00:09:30,661 --> 00:09:34,952
CHERRY_PICK_HEAD and a sequencer directory:
a cherry-pick of several commits.

130
00:09:34,992 --> 00:09:38,777
The todo lists the current pick
first, then the two not yet applied.

131
00:09:39,117 --> 00:09:42,837
"On branch main", and "You are currently
cherry-picking commit a19fbd4".

132
00:09:43,177 --> 00:09:47,750
Repository five. This listing differs
from the previous one in one name.

133
00:09:51,090 --> 00:09:55,113
REVERT_HEAD: a revert, of one
commit, so no sequencer directory.

134
00:09:55,153 --> 00:09:59,239
The prepared message already says
which commit is being reverted.

135
00:09:59,579 --> 00:10:04,742
Repository six. No conflict files here.
Predict the state of the working tree.

136
00:10:08,082 --> 00:10:12,017
Six files that begin with
BISECT. HEAD is a raw commit ID.

137
00:10:12,057 --> 00:10:17,774
BISECT_START names the branch to go back
to. The log is a replayable record, and the

138
00:10:17,814 --> 00:10:21,407
marks are refs under
refs/bisect/: one bad, two good.

139
00:10:21,747 --> 00:10:26,464
"HEAD detached", "You are currently bisecting",
and "nothing to commit, working tree

140
00:10:26,504 --> 00:10:30,597
clean". Nothing looks wrong, and a
commit made now would be on no branch.

141
00:10:30,937 --> 00:10:33,036
One question for six repositories.

142
00:10:33,376 --> 00:10:37,132
One read-only line, and each
repository names its own state.

143
00:10:37,172 --> 00:10:41,878
Note ORIG_HEAD in two of them: a record
of a past command, not a signature.

144
00:10:42,218 --> 00:10:47,121
What Git refuses while an operation is
open. These refusals are often the reported

145
00:10:47,161 --> 00:10:50,918
symptom. Predict the message for
a git switch during the merge.

146
00:10:50,958 --> 00:10:51,883
Say it out loud.

147
00:10:59,723 --> 00:11:04,729
"Cannot switch branch while merging": the
sentence from the hook, and there's the

148
00:11:04,769 --> 00:11:09,776
refusal behind the laptop's "Git is broken".
Each refusal protects the unfinished

149
00:11:09,816 --> 00:11:14,698
state. Read the last message with care.
It proposes rm -fr ".git/rebase-merge".

150
00:11:14,738 --> 00:11:19,682
That deletes the record of where the branch
was and what remains to be replayed.

151
00:11:19,722 --> 00:11:24,604
It's appropriate only when you have
confirmed that no rebase is wanted and have

152
00:11:24,644 --> 00:11:29,588
anchored HEAD first. git rebase --quit
does the same with Git's own bookkeeping.

153
00:11:29,928 --> 00:11:35,117
The ways out. First the one with the red
label. 🔴 DANGEROUS: git merge --abort.

154
00:11:35,157 --> 00:11:40,478
The five answers. What it changes: it ends
the merge and resets the index and the

155
00:11:40,518 --> 00:11:45,839
working tree to HEAD. What it can destroy:
every resolution made so far and edits

156
00:11:45,879 --> 00:11:47,427
staged during the merge.

157
00:11:47,767 --> 00:11:53,110
And uncommitted changes that were present
when the merge started can't always be

158
00:11:53,150 --> 00:11:57,416
reconstructed. Preview: git status
and git diff --cached --stat.

159
00:11:57,456 --> 00:12:01,117
Recovery: staged content
through git fsck --lost-found.

160
00:12:01,157 --> 00:12:06,702
For unstaged resolution edits, none. When
appropriate: when the merge was a mistake

161
00:12:06,742 --> 00:12:08,721
and the preserve step is done.

162
00:12:09,061 --> 00:12:14,176
🟡 CAUTION for the other four: git rebase
--abort, git cherry-pick --abort, git

163
00:12:14,216 --> 00:12:19,529
revert --abort and git bisect reset end
the operation and reset the index and the

164
00:12:19,569 --> 00:12:25,014
working tree to the starting commit. Commits
made inside a rebase leave the branch.

165
00:12:25,054 --> 00:12:29,045
Preview with git status, git
diff and git log <branch>..HEAD.

166
00:12:29,085 --> 00:12:34,530
All five are run here because these sandboxes
hold nothing that was done inside the

167
00:12:34,570 --> 00:12:35,191
operation.

168
00:12:35,531 --> 00:12:39,783
Each repository is back on a branch,
and the count of MERGE files is zero.

169
00:12:39,823 --> 00:12:44,248
The rebase returned to feature/strict-guard,
the branch that head-name named.

170
00:12:44,288 --> 00:12:47,148
The bisect returned to the
branch in BISECT_START.

171
00:12:48,988 --> 00:12:50,636
Five mistakes to watch for.

172
00:12:50,976 --> 00:12:55,887
Concluding that "Git is broken" from a
refusal. Root cause: the refusal is a correct

173
00:12:55,927 --> 00:13:00,308
statement about state files that an
earlier, forgotten command left behind.

174
00:13:00,648 --> 00:13:05,743
Committing during a rebase or a bisect and
expecting the branch to have the commit.

175
00:13:05,783 --> 00:13:10,878
Root cause: HEAD is detached in both
operations, so the new commit extends HEAD and

176
00:13:10,918 --> 00:13:11,496
no branch.

177
00:13:11,836 --> 00:13:16,680
Deleting the state files by hand. Root
cause: the files are both the description of

178
00:13:16,720 --> 00:13:21,269
the operation and its undo record, so
removing them ends the operation without

179
00:13:21,309 --> 00:13:21,916
undoing it.

180
00:13:22,256 --> 00:13:25,507
Aborting without looking for
work done inside the operation.

181
00:13:25,547 --> 00:13:29,951
Root cause: every "leave and restore" exit
resets the index and the working tree,

182
00:13:29,991 --> 00:13:32,914
and commits made on a detached
HEAD lose their anchor.

183
00:13:33,254 --> 00:13:37,974
Treating ORIG_HEAD as a sign of an open
operation. Root cause: it records where a

184
00:13:38,014 --> 00:13:41,677
past command started, and stays
after the command has finished.

185
00:13:43,517 --> 00:13:48,110
Now, out of the lab. A data engineer goes
on leave and hands her laptop's project

186
00:13:48,150 --> 00:13:52,915
directory to a colleague with one line: the
pipeline repository won't let her switch

187
00:13:52,955 --> 00:13:57,377
to the release branch. The colleague
doesn't run a single command that changes

188
00:13:57,417 --> 00:13:59,150
anything for the first minutes.

189
00:13:59,490 --> 00:14:04,341
He lists .git: BISECT_LOG and its companions.
git status confirms it: bisecting,

190
00:14:04,381 --> 00:14:09,478
started from a feature branch, working tree
clean. He reads BISECT_LOG and sees that

191
00:14:09,518 --> 00:14:12,596
she was two steps from the
answer when she stopped.

192
00:14:12,636 --> 00:14:17,610
The reflog, Git's local record of where
HEAD has been, shows one commit made after

193
00:14:17,650 --> 00:14:22,257
the bisect began, on the detached HEAD.
That commit is the one thing in this

194
00:14:22,297 --> 00:14:24,397
repository that an exit could lose.

195
00:14:24,737 --> 00:14:29,599
He gives it a branch name first. Then he
messages her with a precise question, not

196
00:14:29,639 --> 00:14:34,262
"can I reset it?", but: "you were bisecting
from this branch; I have saved the

197
00:14:34,302 --> 00:14:38,686
commit you made on top; do you want
the bisect finished, or may I end it?"

198
00:14:38,726 --> 00:14:43,648
She had forgotten the bisect entirely. He
ends it with git bisect reset, checks the

199
00:14:43,688 --> 00:14:47,294
first line of git status, and
switches to the release branch.

200
00:14:49,134 --> 00:14:56,406
Your turn. Do Lab 35.2, "Five operations
in progress, read from .git alone", in

201
00:14:56,446 --> 00:14:59,646
lab-manual/m35-diagnosis-method.md.

202
00:14:59,986 --> 00:15:04,848
The rule of the lab is in its title: for
each case, name the operation from the

203
00:15:04,888 --> 00:15:09,998
listing of .git before you run git status.
Then predict, in writing, where HEAD is,

204
00:15:10,038 --> 00:15:14,776
which branch will be affected by each
exit, and what each exit would discard.

205
00:15:14,816 --> 00:15:19,926
Only then leave the operation. The lab's
questions are answered in a separate file.

206
00:15:19,966 --> 00:15:21,105
Attempt them first.

207
00:15:21,445 --> 00:15:28,411
